#!/usr/bin/env bash
# reflex-cadence: 4-59/15 * * * *
# reflex-args: --edge
# mesh-storage-health — cross-sense fusion: WHY is this node's storage degraded, not just IS it?
# Combines 3 storage senses into one ATTRIBUTED verdict no single sensor gives.
#
# Inputs (disk-space + disk-latency REQUIRED, best-effort OFFLINE-able; nvme-temp OPTIONAL —
# absent on any non-NVMe node, never penalized as "missing"):
#   mesh-disk-space     .disk-space.state    NORMAL|WARNING|CRITICAL|OFFLINE   (capacity: use%/inode%)
#   mesh-disk-latency   .disk-latency-state  FAST|NORMAL|SLOW|STALLED|UNKNOWN|OFFLINE  (perf: await ms)
#   mesh-nvme-temp      .nvme-temp.state     COOL|WARM|HOT|CRITICAL|OFFLINE    (controller heat)
#   mesh-nvme-health    .nvme-health.state   HEALTHY|DEGRADED|CRITICAL (AGG line)  (MEDIA self-report)
#
# Why this is NEW (not a dup of mesh-system-vitality):
#   mesh-system-vitality folds disk-latency in as ONE of 4 hardware axes (CPU/battery/thermal/disk)
#   — a worst-of aggregate that answers "is the hardware healthy?". It never asks WHY latency
#   degraded, and never looks at capacity or NVMe controller temperature at all.
#   This tool answers the storage-specific follow-up question a sysadmin actually asks when disk
#   is slow: is it explained by something else we already see (near-full capacity triggering SSD
#   garbage-collection slowdown, or a hot NVMe controller throttling), or is it UNATTRIBUTED —
#   which is the most actionable case, because it means neither obvious cause corroborates and the
#   media itself may be failing. Two real, independent physical mechanisms make this a genuine
#   cross-check, not just three numbers glued together:
#     - a near-full SSD loses overprovisioning headroom -> garbage collection stalls -> latency rises
#     - an overheating NVMe controller thermal-throttles reads/writes -> latency rises
#   Latency alone can't tell you which (if either) is the cause; capacity+thermal alone don't know
#   latency degraded at all. Fusing the three gives an ATTRIBUTED verdict, not just a worse number.
#
# THE CROSS-CHECK IS ONLY WORTH ITS NAME WHILE THE AXES STAY INDEPENDENT (2026-08-16, mesh-home):
#   this tool published "CRITICAL — capacity=CRITICAL,latency=STALLED (imminent storage failure)"
#   on a filesystem that was 23% full, writeable, and SMART-clean (spare 99%, media errors 0,
#   critical warning 0x00), and was HEALTHY again 15 minutes later. Both axes were reading ONE
#   fact: mesh-disk-space tested writeability with `timeout 2 touch` and mapped every non-zero rc
#   to "read-only or unwritable", so a write-latency stall (dm-0 write=1392..2199ms in those same
#   minutes) tripped capacity as well as latency, and rule 2 counted the same stall twice. Fixed
#   in mesh-disk-space (probe_write: yes|slow|hung|no — a timeout is not a refusal). The general
#   shape to watch for here: a fused CRITICAL is only evidence if each axis was measured by a
#   mechanism the OTHER axis's fault cannot disturb. Tell in the artifact: that CRITICAL carried
#   no `@<mount>`, because the capacity verdict was not about a full filesystem at all.
#
# LATENCY IS NOT A MEDIA SENSE, AND THIS TOOL SAID "IMMINENT STORAGE FAILURE" OFF IT ALONE
# (2026-08-21, mesh-home, board task {#0af5ca57} — the SECOND false CRITICAL here, and unlike the
# 2026-08-16 one it is NOT a shared-input artifact: capacity carried its @/ mount, was NORMAL@18%,
# thermal COOL. latency=STALLED was the sole trigger and it fired the incident by itself):
#   /proc/diskstats write_await = write_ticks/writes_completed, and a request's ticks accrue from
#   QUEUE INSERT to completion. It is therefore a QUEUE-INCLUSIVE number that measures OFFERED LOAD
#   at least as much as the device, and its denominator varies, so a few large queued writes give a
#   huge mean while a heavy steady stream dilutes it. MEASURED on this node the same morning,
#   against `smartctl -A` taken in the same run (critical_warning 0x00, available_spare 99%,
#   media_and_data_integrity_errors 0, error_information_log_entries 0 — both NVMe, PASSED):
#     idle 10s          dm-0 write_await   0.09ms   device service time 0.17ms/write
#     one 2GB dd+fsync  dm-0 write_await 254.49ms   device service time 0.33ms/write
#     64x256MB writers  dm-0 write_await 187.77ms   device service time 4.34ms/write
#   — a 2800x swing on the axis this tool alarms on, while the device's own service time moved 2x.
#   And STALLED itself REPRODUCES ON DEMAND on that clean drive: `mesh-disk-latency` run right
#   after an rm+sync of a few GB of scratch read "STALLED write=1175.37ms", while the SAME tool run
#   DURING a real 6GB write read only SLOW (152ms) — so the band does not even track load
#   monotonically, it tracks the interval's IO mix. Nothing about the media was wrong in any of it.
# THE FIX, and note its ASYMMETRY: latency=STALLED as the SOLE trigger is downgraded to DEGRADED
#   only when a FRESH MEDIA READ POSITIVELY CONTRADICTS it (mesh-nvme-health AGG=HEALTHY — the
#   controller's own wear/error self-report, an axis no amount of queueing can disturb). Missing or
#   stale media evidence NEVER downgrades: no reading -> CRITICAL stands, and the reason says
#   "media=unread" so the absence is visible instead of being spent as an all-clear.
# AND PERSISTENCE STILL ESCALATES: a load burst does not survive its interval (both wild CRITICALs
#   here were HEALTHY again on the very next fire), a dying drive does. STALLED held for
#   STALL_ESCALATE consecutive evals re-raises CRITICAL even against a HEALTHY media report, so the
#   downgrade costs the incident tag on a transient and nothing on a real hang.
#
# Bands (FALSIFIABLE: change a band rule -> test flips):
#   CRITICAL   capacity=CRITICAL (exhausted/read-only) OR thermal=CRITICAL OR media=CRITICAL
#              — imminent storage failure regardless of attribution
#              latency=STALLED also raises CRITICAL, UNLESS it is the sole trigger AND media reads
#              a fresh HEALTHY (then DEGRADED — see the falsification note above), UNLESS it has
#              persisted STALL_ESCALATE consecutive evals (then CRITICAL again, persistence-attributed)
#   DEGRADED   capacity=WARNING OR latency=SLOW OR thermal=HOT
#              — reason names the ATTRIBUTION: thermal-attributed / capacity-attributed / BOTH /
#                UNATTRIBUTED (latency degraded, neither capacity nor thermal corroborates —
#                flagged as the highest-priority case: investigate media health directly)
#   HEALTHY    capacity=NORMAL AND latency in {FAST,NORMAL} AND thermal in {COOL,WARM,n/a}
#   DEGRADED   both required inputs OFFLINE (can't assess at all)
#   DEGRADED   1 required input OFFLINE (partial — names which)
#   (thermal OFFLINE is NEVER counted as missing — a SATA-only node has no NVMe controller to
#   read, that is a legitimate permanent state, not a sensor failure; reported as thermal=n/a)
#
#   mesh-storage-health          one-shot verdict to stdout
#   mesh-storage-health --edge   emit only on verdict change (cron reflex)
#   mesh-storage-health --json   machine-readable {label, reason, inputs, ts}
#   mesh-storage-health --test   smoke test (pure classifier, no state-file reads)
set -uo pipefail
export PATH="$HOME/.local/bin:$PATH"
[ -n "${HOME:-}" ] || HOME=$(getent passwd "$(id -u)" 2>/dev/null | cut -d: -f6); export HOME

MESH="${MESH_DIR:-$HOME/.mesh}"
STATE="$MESH/.storage-health-state"
STALE_S=1800  # 30min — generous vs the slowest input cadence (disk-space/nvme-temp */15)
# the MEDIA axis has its own bound: mesh-nvme-health is a `0 */6` reflex (a SMART read is expensive
# and wear moves in days, not minutes). Judging it by STALE_S would render it stale on every single
# eval — an axis permanently n/a is an axis that is not in the fusion at all.
MEDIA_STALE_S=28800   # 8h — 6h cadence + 2h slack

# COLD START — AN AGE MEASURED ACROSS A POWER-OFF IS NOT A SILENCE (2026-08-29, health).
# This node power-cycles several times a day. The first storage-health fire after a boot ran 76s
# into uptime against a .disk-space.state last written 6h52m earlier — before the previous
# shutdown — scored both required axes OFFLINE and published DEGRADED "can't assess storage".
# Reproduced 3/3 boots (08-27T15:19, 08-28T07:19, 08-29T07:49, each ~60s after its boot), each
# healing on its own one cadence later. Nothing was wrong with the disks: the feeders had not been
# ALIVE to miss their cadence, the machine had been off. An unpowered feeder is not a dead one, and
# DEGRADED is an alarm word ABOUT STORAGE.
# THE HOLD IS BOUNDED BY THE SAME ARITHMETIC THAT DECLARES THE STALENESS, so it needs no new
# constant and cannot outlive its own premise: for the first STALE_S of UPTIME an absent or
# pre-boot artifact reads COLDSTART, an ABSTENTION that classifies to UNKNOWN and is deliberately
# outside mesh-dash's _STATE_ALARM_RE (gated against the dash's own regex, with a control arm).
# Past STALE_S of uptime the feeder has had its full window and the identical absence reads
# OFFLINE again — so the quiet SELF-EXPIRES into the loud DEGRADED and a genuinely dead feeder can
# never hide behind a boot. A real OFFLINE standing beside a COLDSTART still wins: one feeder
# writing garbage this boot is a fault, and a reboot must not launder it.
# NOT excused: a file written THIS boot. It is judged by its own age exactly as before.

# coldstart_p <uptime_s> <now_s> <mtime_s|""> -> 0 when the artifact cannot yet be evidence of a
# dead feeder. Pure arithmetic so the boot grace is testable without faking /proc or the clock.
coldstart_p() {
  local up="${1:-}" now="${2:-0}" m="${3:-}"
  # an unreadable uptime must never MINT a cold start: blindness may not buy silence
  case "$up" in ''|*[!0-9]*) return 1 ;; esac
  [ "$up" -lt "$STALE_S" ] || return 1
  [ -n "$m" ] || return 0
  [ "$m" -lt $(( now - up )) ]
}
_uptime_s() { local u; u="$(cut -d' ' -f1 /proc/uptime 2>/dev/null)"; printf '%s' "${u%%.*}"; }
_in_coldstart() {
  local f="$1" m=""
  [ ! -f "$f" ] || m="$(stat -c %Y "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
  coldstart_p "$(_uptime_s)" "$(date +%s)" "$m"
}
STALL_ESCALATE=3      # consecutive evals of latency=STALLED that re-raise CRITICAL despite healthy
                      # media (at the 4-59/15 cadence: 45min — longer than any burst seen here)

# --- read helpers (required axes return OFFLINE on missing/stale/unreadable) ---
read_capacity() {
  local f="$1" v now m
  # absent or aged-out: OFFLINE means "this feeder is dead", so only claim it once the feeder has
  # actually had a window to run in (see the COLD START block above) — never across a power-off.
  [ -f "$f" ] || { _in_coldstart "$f" && { echo "COLDSTART"; return; }; echo "OFFLINE"; return; }
  now=$(date +%s); m=$(stat -c %Y "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo "$now")
  if [ $((now - m)) -gt "$STALE_S" ]; then
    _in_coldstart "$f" && { echo "COLDSTART"; return; }
    echo "OFFLINE"; return
  fi
  # an UNPARSEABLE value in a file written this boot is a real fault and stays OFFLINE
  v="$(head -1 "$f" 2>/dev/null | cut -d'|' -f1)"
  case "$v" in
    NORMAL|WARNING|CRITICAL) printf '%s' "$v" ;;
    *) echo "OFFLINE" ;;
  esac
}
# WHICH filesystem is the capacity claim ABOUT? (2026-08-15 phaedra incident): this fusion said
# 'HEALTHY|capacity=NORMAL' on a box whose /tmp was 100% full and refusing every write, because the
# capacity axis was a claim about the ROOT fs and nothing said so. mesh-disk-space now scans every
# mount and names the worst one in its reason ("use=NN% on <mount>"); carry that name up so the
# verdict is capacity=<label>@<mount> — a reader can no longer take it for the fs they had in mind.
read_capacity_where() {
  local f="$1" w=""
  [ -f "$f" ] || { printf ''; return; }
  w="$(head -1 "$f" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/.*use=[0-9]*% on \([^ )|]*\).*/\1/p')"
  printf '%s' "$w"
}
read_latency() {
  local f="$1" v now m
  # absent or aged-out: OFFLINE means "this feeder is dead", so only claim it once the feeder has
  # actually had a window to run in (see the COLD START block above) — never across a power-off.
  [ -f "$f" ] || { _in_coldstart "$f" && { echo "COLDSTART"; return; }; echo "OFFLINE"; return; }
  now=$(date +%s); m=$(stat -c %Y "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo "$now")
  if [ $((now - m)) -gt "$STALE_S" ]; then
    _in_coldstart "$f" && { echo "COLDSTART"; return; }
    echo "OFFLINE"; return
  fi
  # an UNPARSEABLE value in a file written this boot is a real fault and stays OFFLINE
  v="$(head -1 "$f" 2>/dev/null | cut -d'|' -f1)"
  case "$v" in
    FAST|NORMAL|SLOW|STALLED|UNKNOWN) printf '%s' "$v" ;;
    *) echo "OFFLINE" ;;
  esac
}
# thermal is OPTIONAL: no readable state -> "n/a" (never "OFFLINE"/missing — no NVMe is legitimate)
read_thermal() {
  local f="$1" v now m
  [ -f "$f" ] || { echo "n/a"; return; }
  now=$(date +%s); m=$(stat -c %Y "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo "$now")
  if [ $((now - m)) -gt "$STALE_S" ]; then echo "n/a"; return; fi
  v="$(head -1 "$f" 2>/dev/null | cut -d'|' -f1)"
  case "$v" in
    COOL|WARM|HOT|CRITICAL) printf '%s' "$v" ;;
    *) echo "n/a" ;;
  esac
}

# MEDIA is the axis that answers the question the CRITICAL band's words actually make ("imminent
# storage FAILURE"): the controller's own wear/error self-report, which queueing cannot disturb.
# OPTIONAL like thermal — a node with no NVMe / no sudo / no smartctl legitimately has none, so a
# missing read is "n/a", never "OFFLINE". n/a must never be spent as an all-clear (see classify).
read_media() {
  local f="$1" v now m
  [ -f "$f" ] || { echo "n/a"; return; }
  now=$(date +%s); m=$(stat -c %Y "$f" 2>/dev/null || echo "$now")
  if [ $((now - m)) -gt "$MEDIA_STALE_S" ]; then echo "n/a"; return; fi
  # mesh-nvme-health writes an AGG roll-up line plus one line per device; the AGG line is the
  # fleet-of-one verdict. Grep it by name rather than by position — a per-device line first would
  # otherwise silently become the whole node's media verdict.
  v="$(grep -m1 '^AGG:' "$f" 2>/dev/null | sed -n 's/^AGG:\([A-Z]*\)|.*/\1/p')"
  case "$v" in
    HEALTHY|DEGRADED|CRITICAL) printf '%s' "$v" ;;
    *) echo "n/a" ;;
  esac
}

# --- classifier (pure, offline-testable) ---
# classify_storage <capacity> <latency> <thermal> [capwhere] [media] [stallrun] -> "LABEL|reason"
#   media    HEALTHY|DEGRADED|CRITICAL|n/a  — optional, defaults n/a (behaves as before when absent)
#   stallrun integer — how many consecutive evals latency has now read STALLED, this one included
classify_storage() {
  local cap="$1" lat="$2" therm="$3" capwhere="${4:-}" media="${5:-n/a}" stallrun="${6:-0}"
  # every 'capacity=<label>' in a reason carries its MOUNT when the capacity sense named one
  local cap_d="$cap${capwhere:+@$capwhere}"
  # 0. COLD-START ABSTENTION — checked BEFORE the missing tally, and any genuine OFFLINE beside it
  #    still wins, so a reboot cannot launder a feeder that is really dead. The reason PROSE must
  #    carry no band word: the wall matches on text, so quoting one here would alarm the row anyway
  #    and undo the whole retype (gated in --test against mesh-dash's own regex).
  if [ "$cap" != "OFFLINE" ] && [ "$lat" != "OFFLINE" ] \
     && { [ "$cap" = "COLDSTART" ] || [ "$lat" = "COLDSTART" ]; }; then
    local cold=""
    [ "$cap" = "COLDSTART" ] && cold="${cold}disk-space,"
    [ "$lat" = "COLDSTART" ] && cold="${cold}disk-latency,"
    echo "UNKNOWN|coldstart: ${cold%,} (no reading since this boot — the machine power-cycled and these feeders have not reached their cadence yet, so nothing is asserted about storage either way; the abstention self-expires after ${STALE_S}s of uptime, when the same absence is judged as a missing input) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
    return
  fi
  # 1. Missing-required tally (thermal excluded — optional axis)
  local missing="" degen=0
  [ "$cap" = "OFFLINE" ] && { missing="${missing}disk-space," ; degen=$((degen+1)); }
  [ "$lat" = "OFFLINE" ] && { missing="${missing}disk-latency," ; degen=$((degen+1)); }
  missing="${missing%,}"
  if [ "$degen" -ge 2 ]; then
    echo "DEGRADED|missing: $missing (both required inputs offline — can't assess storage)"
    return
  fi
  if [ "$degen" -eq 1 ]; then
    echo "DEGRADED|missing: $missing (1 required input offline, can't fully assert) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
    return
  fi
  # 1b. MEDIA=CRITICAL is its own trigger — the controller predicting its own failure needs no
  #     corroboration from a performance number.
  # 2. CRITICAL: capacity exhausted, thermal at/past crit, or media critical — regardless of attribution
  if [ "$cap" = "CRITICAL" ] || [ "$therm" = "CRITICAL" ] || [ "$media" = "CRITICAL" ]; then
    local why=""
    [ "$cap" = "CRITICAL" ] && why="${why}capacity=CRITICAL,"
    [ "$lat" = "STALLED" ] && why="${why}latency=STALLED,"
    [ "$therm" = "CRITICAL" ] && why="${why}thermal=CRITICAL,"
    [ "$media" = "CRITICAL" ] && why="${why}media=CRITICAL,"
    echo "CRITICAL|${why%,} (imminent storage failure) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
    return
  fi
  # 2b. latency=STALLED. Queue-inclusive await is not a media sense (see the header measurement),
  #     so what this branch decides is WHETHER ANYTHING ELSE AGREES the storage is failing.
  if [ "$lat" = "STALLED" ]; then
    local corrob=""
    [ "$cap" = "WARNING" ] && corrob="${corrob}capacity,"
    [ "$therm" = "HOT" ] && corrob="${corrob}thermal,"
    [ "$media" = "DEGRADED" ] && corrob="${corrob}media,"
    if [ -n "$corrob" ]; then
      echo "CRITICAL|latency=STALLED corroborated=${corrob%,} (imminent storage failure) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
      return
    fi
    # persistence beats a healthy self-report: a load burst does not survive its own interval.
    if [ "$stallrun" -ge "$STALL_ESCALATE" ]; then
      echo "CRITICAL|latency=STALLED persisted ${stallrun} consecutive evals (past a load burst — escalated despite media=$media) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
      return
    fi
    # the ONLY downgrade, and it needs POSITIVE contradicting evidence, never mere absence
    if [ "$media" = "HEALTHY" ]; then
      echo "DEGRADED|latency=STALLED UNATTRIBUTED but media=HEALTHY CONTRADICTS it (SMART self-report clean; diskstats await is queue-inclusive — reads as load, not failure). Escalates to CRITICAL if held ${STALL_ESCALATE} evals | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
      return
    fi
    # media unread/stale/absent: absence of evidence is NOT evidence of health — CRITICAL stands,
    # and the reason SAYS the axis was unread so nobody mistakes it for a checked all-clear.
    echo "CRITICAL|latency=STALLED, media=unread (no SMART corroborate — media health not ruled out) | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
    return
  fi
  # 3. DEGRADED: capacity warning, latency slow, or thermal hot — ATTRIBUTE if latency is the trigger
  if [ "$cap" = "WARNING" ] || [ "$lat" = "SLOW" ] || [ "$therm" = "HOT" ] || [ "$media" = "DEGRADED" ]; then
    local attrib=""
    if [ "$lat" = "SLOW" ]; then
      local cause=""
      [ "$therm" = "HOT" ] && cause="${cause}thermal,"
      [ "$cap" = "WARNING" ] && cause="${cause}capacity,"
      [ "$media" = "DEGRADED" ] && cause="${cause}media,"
      if [ -z "$cause" ]; then
        # the media axis exists now, so "investigate media health directly" must not be printed
        # when it ALREADY WAS: an instruction to check something already checked is a reader
        # spending their turn re-deriving a fact the line is holding.
        if [ "$media" = "HEALTHY" ]; then
          attrib="latency=SLOW UNATTRIBUTED, media=HEALTHY already checked (SMART clean — queue-inclusive await reads as load, not media)"
        else
          attrib="latency=SLOW UNATTRIBUTED (no thermal/capacity correlate, media=$media — investigate media health directly)"
        fi
      else
        attrib="latency=SLOW attributed=${cause%,} (corroborated cause found)"
      fi
    elif [ "$media" = "DEGRADED" ]; then
      attrib="media=DEGRADED (drive self-report — the axis queueing cannot fake)"
    elif [ "$therm" = "HOT" ]; then
      attrib="thermal=HOT (leading indicator — latency not yet degraded)"
    else
      attrib="capacity=WARNING${capwhere:+ on $capwhere} (latency not yet degraded)"
    fi
    echo "DEGRADED|$attrib | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"
    return
  fi
  # 4. HEALTHY: capacity NORMAL, latency FAST/NORMAL, thermal COOL/WARM/n/a, media HEALTHY/n/a
  #    ...and ONLY those. This was an unconditional fall-through until 2026-08-29, so ANY axis word
  #    the bands above did not recognise arrived here and was published as "storage nominal" — a
  #    faked all-clear minted by the parser, the one direction honest fusion may never fail in.
  #    Found by the COLDSTART gates: classify_storage COLDSTART COLDSTART n/a read HEALTHY.
  case "$cap" in NORMAL) ;; *) echo "UNKNOWN|unrecognised capacity axis value '$cap' — no band claims it, and an unclaimed value may not be spent as an all-clear | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"; return ;; esac
  # latency=UNKNOWN is a CLAIMED value, not an unrecognised one: mesh-disk-latency emits it for
  # "ran, but there was no IO to measure", and :353 has treated it as non-degraded since long
  # before this gate. It is a different namespace from the UNKNOWN *label* this classifier now
  # returns as an abstention — do not collapse the two. (Whether an axis that could not measure
  # should reach HEALTHY at all is a real and separate question; it is flagged, not changed here.)
  case "$lat" in FAST|NORMAL|UNKNOWN) ;; *) echo "UNKNOWN|unrecognised latency axis value '$lat' — no band claims it, and an unclaimed value may not be spent as an all-clear | capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media"; return ;; esac
  echo "HEALTHY|capacity=$cap_d latency=$lat thermal=$therm media=$media (storage nominal)"
}

# --- Test mode (pure classifier, no state-file reads) ---
if [ "${1:-}" = --test ]; then
  fail=0
  # CRITICAL — each trigger alone
  r="$(classify_storage CRITICAL FAST COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL CRITICAL-capacity: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL CRITICAL-latency: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST CRITICAL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL CRITICAL-thermal: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED with attribution: thermal corroborates slow latency
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL SLOW HOT)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-thermal-attrib label: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'attributed=thermal' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-thermal-attrib reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED with attribution: capacity corroborates slow latency
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING SLOW COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-capacity-attrib label: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'attributed=capacity' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-capacity-attrib reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED with attribution: BOTH corroborate
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING SLOW HOT)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'attributed=thermal,capacity\|attributed=capacity,thermal' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-both-attrib: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED UNATTRIBUTED: slow latency, nothing corroborates — the actionable case
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL SLOW COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'UNATTRIBUTED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-unattributed: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED: thermal HOT alone (latency not yet degraded) — leading indicator
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST HOT)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-thermal-leading label: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'leading indicator' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-thermal-leading reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED: capacity WARNING alone
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING FAST COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-capacity-alone: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # HEALTHY: all nominal
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' || { echo "  FAIL HEALTHY: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # HEALTHY: thermal n/a (no NVMe hardware) is a valid steady-state, never penalized
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL NORMAL n/a)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' || { echo "  FAIL HEALTHY-no-nvme: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # HEALTHY: latency UNKNOWN (no IO yet) treated as non-degraded (never falls into SLOW/STALLED branch)
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL UNKNOWN COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' || { echo "  FAIL HEALTHY-latency-unknown: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # CRITICAL beats DEGRADED in priority
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING STALLED HOT)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL CRITICAL-priority: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED: both required OFFLINE
  r="$(classify_storage OFFLINE OFFLINE COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-2-offline: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'disk-space' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-2-offline detail: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # DEGRADED: 1 required OFFLINE (thermal n/a doesn't count, only 1 total missing)
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL OFFLINE n/a)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-1-offline: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'disk-latency' || { echo "  FAIL DEGRADED-1-offline detail: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # --- COLD START: an age measured across a POWER-OFF is not a silence (2026-08-29) ---
  # This node power-cycles. The first fire after a boot ran 76s into uptime against feeder state
  # files last written before the previous shutdown, scored both required axes OFFLINE, and
  # published DEGRADED "can't assess storage" — an alarm word about DISKS for a machine whose disks
  # were fine and whose feeders simply had not reached their cadence yet. 3/3 boots reproduced it.
  # (a) both axes cold -> UNKNOWN. An abstention is not a verdict about storage.
  r="$(classify_storage COLDSTART COLDSTART n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^UNKNOWN' || { echo "  FAIL coldstart-both label (a boot must not publish a storage verdict): got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'disk-space,disk-latency' || { echo "  FAIL coldstart-both must name both cold feeders: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (b) THE SELF-EXPIRY that makes the quiet safe: the identical absence, once uptime has passed a
  #     whole staleness window, is a genuinely dead feeder and must still be DEGRADED. Retyping this
  #     one would trade a false alarm for a silent dead feeder, which is the worse direction.
  r="$(classify_storage OFFLINE OFFLINE n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL offline-both must stay DEGRADED (a dead feeder cannot inherit the boot excuse): got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (c) a real dead feeder standing BESIDE a cold one is still a fault — a boot must not launder it
  r="$(classify_storage COLDSTART OFFLINE n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL cold+offline must be DEGRADED: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'disk-latency' || { echo "  FAIL cold+offline must name the DEAD feeder, not the cold one: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (d) one cold axis alone still abstains: nothing is KNOWN to be wrong, so no band may be spent
  r="$(classify_storage COLDSTART FAST n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^UNKNOWN' || { echo "  FAIL coldstart-single: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (e) THE WORD MUST BE QUIET ON THE WALL — and the gate is mesh-dash's OWN _STATE_ALARM_RE, read
  #     from the dash itself, never a copy of it here: a predicate that is not the reader's own
  #     predicate is not a guard on that reader. The whole point of the retype is that this row
  #     stops waking a human every boot; if the reason PROSE quotes a band word the row alarms
  #     anyway and nothing was fixed. Tested against the real emitted text, plus the axis fields as
  #     the state file assembles them. Control arm below, or (e) proves nothing.
  _dre="$(sed -n "s/^_STATE_ALARM_RE='\\(.*\\)'$/\\1/p" "$(command -v mesh-dash 2>/dev/null || echo scripts/mesh-dash)" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
  if [ -n "$_dre" ]; then
    printf 'DEGRADED|missing: disk-space,disk-latency\n' | grep -qE "$_dre" \
      || { echo "  FAIL alarm-alphabet CONTROL arm dead: mesh-dash's regex no longer matches the band it is supposed to, so the quiet check below proves nothing"; fail=1; }
    _cl="$(classify_storage COLDSTART COLDSTART n/a)"
    printf '%s\n' "$_cl" | grep -qE "$_dre" \
      && { echo "  FAIL the coldstart verdict matches mesh-dash's alarm alphabet — the abstention wakes a reader exactly like the fault it replaces: got=$_cl"; fail=1; }
    printf 'UNKNOWN|capacity=COLDSTART|latency=COLDSTART|thermal=n/a|media=n/a|stall=0\n' | grep -qE "$_dre" \
      && { echo "  FAIL the coldstart AXIS FIELDS alarm on the wall even though the label does not"; fail=1; }
  else
    echo "  NOTE alarm-alphabet gate SKIPPED: could not read _STATE_ALARM_RE out of mesh-dash"; fail=1
  fi
  # (i) THE WIRING, not just the parts: the read helpers are what actually turn a pre-boot file
  #     into an axis value, and every gate above would stay green if the COLDSTART branch were
  #     deleted from them. Driven over real files with real mtimes, asserting BOTH directions so
  #     the arm is honest on a long-uptime node instead of silently skipping.
  _rw="$(mktemp -d)"
  printf 'NORMAL|use=11%% on /\n' > "$_rw/cap"; printf 'FAST|await=1ms\n' > "$_rw/lat"
  touch -d '1970-01-02 00:00:00' "$_rw/cap" "$_rw/lat"
  _rwc="$(read_capacity "$_rw/cap")"; _rwl="$(read_latency "$_rw/lat")"
  _rwa="$(read_capacity "$_rw/absent")"
  rm -rf "$_rw"
  if [ "$(_uptime_s)" -lt "$STALE_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    [ "$_rwc" = COLDSTART ] && [ "$_rwl" = COLDSTART ] && [ "$_rwa" = COLDSTART ] \
      || { echo "  FAIL read helpers do not carry the boot grace (cap=$_rwc lat=$_rwl absent=$_rwa; want COLDSTART x3) — the classifier band is wired to nothing"; fail=1; }
  else
    [ "$_rwc" = OFFLINE ] && [ "$_rwl" = OFFLINE ] && [ "$_rwa" = OFFLINE ] \
      || { echo "  FAIL read helpers excused a stale file past the grace (cap=$_rwc lat=$_rwl absent=$_rwa; want OFFLINE x3)"; fail=1; }
  fi
  # (h) HEALTHY may never be the fall-through for a word no band claims. Until 2026-08-29 it was,
  #     so an unrecognised axis value published "storage nominal" — a faked all-clear minted by the
  #     parser itself. Driven with a word that is deliberately not in any band's alphabet.
  r="$(classify_storage BOGUSCAP FAST n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' && { echo "  FAIL unrecognised capacity value published an all-clear: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL BOGUSLAT n/a)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' && { echo "  FAIL unrecognised latency value published an all-clear: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # ...and the real HEALTHY still reaches HEALTHY (control: without this the gate above is trivially
  # satisfied by a classifier that never says HEALTHY at all)
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^HEALTHY' || { echo "  FAIL nominal storage no longer reaches HEALTHY — the fall-through fix ate the healthy band: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (f) the pure boot-grace arithmetic: <uptime> <now> <mtime|"" for absent>
  coldstart_p 76 1000000 999000 || { echo "  FAIL coldstart_p: a pre-boot artifact 76s into uptime is not evidence of a dead feeder"; fail=1; }
  coldstart_p 76 1000000 "" || { echo "  FAIL coldstart_p: an absent artifact 76s into uptime"; fail=1; }
  coldstart_p 76 1000000 999990 && { echo "  FAIL coldstart_p: an artifact written AFTER boot was excused as a cold start — it must be judged by its own age"; fail=1; }
  coldstart_p $((STALE_S + 1)) 1000000 1 && { echo "  FAIL coldstart_p: the boot excuse outlived the staleness window — a dead feeder would hide behind it forever"; fail=1; }
  coldstart_p "" 1000000 1 && { echo "  FAIL coldstart_p: an unreadable uptime minted a cold start (blindness must never buy silence)"; fail=1; }
  # (g) the ADAPTER over real files: same two outcomes, driven through stat/mtime, not arithmetic
  _cs="$(mktemp -d)"; : > "$_cs/fresh"; touch -d '1970-01-02 00:00:00' "$_cs/old"
  _in_coldstart "$_cs/absent" ; _r1=$?
  _in_coldstart "$_cs/old"    ; _r2=$?
  _in_coldstart "$_cs/fresh"  ; _r3=$?
  rm -rf "$_cs"
  if [ "$(_uptime_s)" -lt "$STALE_S" ] 2>/dev/null; then
    [ "$_r1" = 0 ] && [ "$_r2" = 0 ] && [ "$_r3" != 0 ] \
      || { echo "  FAIL _in_coldstart on real files inside the grace (absent=$_r1 pre-boot=$_r2 this-boot=$_r3; want 0 0 non-0)"; fail=1; }
  else
    [ "$_r1" != 0 ] && [ "$_r2" != 0 ] && [ "$_r3" != 0 ] \
      || { echo "  FAIL _in_coldstart past the grace must excuse NOTHING (absent=$_r1 pre-boot=$_r2 this-boot=$_r3; want all non-0)"; fail=1; }
  fi
  # thermal n/a (missing) never counts toward the required-offline tally even combined with a real offline
  r="$(classify_storage OFFLINE NORMAL n/a)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL thermal-not-counted label: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'disk-latency' && { echo "  FAIL thermal-not-counted: should only list disk-space, got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # --- MOUNT ATTRIBUTION (2026-08-15): the verdict must say WHICH filesystem the capacity claim is
  # about. 'capacity=NORMAL' was published on a box whose /tmp was full and whose ROOT fs was fine.
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL /tmp)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'capacity=NORMAL@/tmp' || { echo "  FAIL attribution-healthy: capacity claim does not name its mount: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage CRITICAL FAST COOL /tmp)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'capacity=CRITICAL@/tmp' || { echo "  FAIL attribution-critical: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING FAST COOL /dev/shm)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'on /dev/shm' || { echo "  FAIL attribution-warning-alone: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # a capacity sense that named no mount must NOT invent one (no stray '@')
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'capacity=NORMAL@' && { echo "  FAIL attribution-absent: invented a mount: got=$r"; fail=1; }

  # --- MEDIA AXIS (2026-08-21, board {#0af5ca57}) — the asymmetry is the whole fix, so each arm
  # is asserted separately. A gate you have not seen FAIL is not a gate: flipping the DEGRADED in
  # the media branch to CRITICAL, or dropping the stallrun escalation, must turn these red.
  # (a) STALLED alone + a FRESH HEALTHY media self-report -> DEGRADED, not an incident
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL / HEALTHY 1)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL media-contradicts-stall label: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'imminent storage failure' && { echo "  FAIL media-contradicts-stall: still claims imminent failure: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'media=HEALTHY CONTRADICTS' || { echo "  FAIL media-contradicts-stall reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (b) ABSENCE OF EVIDENCE NEVER DOWNGRADES: media unread -> CRITICAL stands and SAYS it is unread
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL / n/a 1)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL media-unread-must-not-downgrade: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'media=unread' || { echo "  FAIL media-unread must name the missing axis: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (c) PERSISTENCE BEATS A CLEAN SMART: a burst does not survive its interval, a dying drive does
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL / HEALTHY "$STALL_ESCALATE")"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL stall-persistence-escalation: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'persisted' || { echo "  FAIL stall-persistence reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL / HEALTHY "$((STALL_ESCALATE - 1))")"
  echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL stall-escalation boundary (one below must NOT escalate): got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (d) a corroborating axis restores the incident immediately, no persistence needed
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL / DEGRADED 1)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL stall+media-degraded: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage WARNING STALLED COOL / HEALTHY 1)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL stall+capacity-warning: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED HOT / HEALTHY 1)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL stall+thermal-hot: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (e) media is a trigger in its own right — the controller predicting its own death needs no second opinion
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL / CRITICAL 0)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL media-critical-alone: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL / DEGRADED 0)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^DEGRADED' || { echo "  FAIL media-degraded-alone: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'media=DEGRADED (drive self-report' || { echo "  FAIL media-degraded-alone attribution: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (f) BACKWARD COMPAT: called with the old 3/4-arg signature (media defaults n/a) the CRITICAL
  #     band must still fire on STALLED — no caller silently loses its alarm.
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL STALLED COOL)"; echo "$r" | grep -q '^CRITICAL' || { echo "  FAIL legacy-signature-stall: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # (g) the verdict must PUBLISH the media axis so a reader can never mistake n/a for checked-clean
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL FAST COOL / n/a 0)"; echo "$r" | grep -q 'media=n/a' || { echo "  FAIL media not published in HEALTHY reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }

  # (h) SLOW UNATTRIBUTED must not order a check the fusion already performed
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL SLOW COOL / HEALTHY 0)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'investigate media health directly' && { echo "  FAIL slow-unattributed: orders a media check it already has the answer to: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'media=HEALTHY already checked' || { echo "  FAIL slow-unattributed reason: got=$r"; fail=1; }
  # ...but with NO media reading it must still send the reader to the media, and name the absence
  r="$(classify_storage NORMAL SLOW COOL / n/a 0)"
  echo "$r" | grep -q 'investigate media health directly' || { echo "  FAIL slow-unattributed-no-media: got=$r"; fail=1; }

  # --- MEDIA STALENESS BOUND: judging a `0 */6` reflex by STALE_S (30min) renders it n/a on every
  # eval — an axis permanently absent is an axis not in the fusion, and here that silently restores
  # the exact false CRITICAL this change removes. Assert the bound covers the producer's cadence.
  _nh="$(dirname "$0")/mesh-nvme-health"; [ -f "$_nh" ] || _nh="$(command -v mesh-nvme-health 2>/dev/null)"
  if [ -n "$_nh" ] && [ -f "$_nh" ]; then
    _cad="$(grep -m1 '^# reflex-cadence:' "$_nh" | sed -n 's/.*reflex-cadence: *[0-9*]* \*\/\([0-9]\+\) .*/\1/p')"
    if [ -n "$_cad" ]; then
      [ "$MEDIA_STALE_S" -gt $(( _cad * 3600 )) ] || { echo "  FAIL media-stale-bound: MEDIA_STALE_S=$MEDIA_STALE_S <= mesh-nvme-health cadence ${_cad}h — the axis would read n/a on every eval"; fail=1; }
    else
      echo "  (media-stale-bound gate skipped: could not parse mesh-nvme-health's cadence)"
    fi
  else
    echo "  (media-stale-bound gate skipped: mesh-nvme-health not present)"
  fi

  # --- CROSS-TOOL FORMAT GATE for the media axis: read_media must parse the bytes mesh-nvme-health
  # ACTUALLY writes, not a fixture agreeing with itself. Copy the LIVE state into a sandbox and
  # re-stamp its mtime — never read the live path through the staleness branch (a genuinely old
  # file would fail this gate for a reason that is not a format drift), and never WRITE the live
  # artifact from a --test.
  if [ -f "$MESH/.nvme-health.state" ]; then
    _mb="$(mktemp -d -t mesh-storage-health-media.XXXXXXXX)"
    cp "$MESH/.nvme-health.state" "$_mb/live.state" && touch "$_mb/live.state"
    _mv="$(read_media "$_mb/live.state")"
    case "$_mv" in
      HEALTHY|DEGRADED|CRITICAL) : ;;
      *) echo "  FAIL cross-tool media: mesh-nvme-health's live state parses to '$_mv' — format drift, the axis is silently absent: $(head -1 "$_mb/live.state")"; fail=1 ;;
    esac
    # a state file carrying ONLY per-device lines must NOT have one device promoted to the node verdict
    grep -v '^AGG:' "$_mb/live.state" > "$_mb/nodeagg.state" 2>/dev/null || true
    if [ -s "$_mb/nodeagg.state" ]; then
      touch "$_mb/nodeagg.state"
      _mv2="$(read_media "$_mb/nodeagg.state")"
      [ "$_mv2" = "n/a" ] || { echo "  FAIL cross-tool media: a per-device line was read as the node verdict ('$_mv2')"; fail=1; }
    fi
    # stale beyond the bound -> n/a (and n/a must never downgrade — asserted in (b))
    touch -d '-9 hours' "$_mb/live.state" 2>/dev/null && {
      [ "$(read_media "$_mb/live.state")" = "n/a" ] || { echo "  FAIL media staleness not enforced at 9h"; fail=1; }
    }
    rm -rf "$_mb"
  else
    echo "  (cross-tool media format gate skipped: no .nvme-health.state on this node)"
  fi

  # --- CROSS-TOOL FORMAT GATE: read_capacity_where parses the reason string that mesh-disk-space
  # ACTUALLY writes. Parsing a hand-written fixture would assert only that this file agrees with
  # itself, and the two could drift the day the producer's wording changes. Drive the real producer
  # into a sandbox HOME (never the live state file — a --test must not write the artifact it checks).
  _ds=""; [ -x "$(dirname "$0")/mesh-disk-space" ] && _ds="$(dirname "$0")/mesh-disk-space"
  [ -z "$_ds" ] && command -v mesh-disk-space >/dev/null 2>&1 && _ds="$(command -v mesh-disk-space)"
  if [ -n "$_ds" ]; then
    _sb="$(mktemp -d -t mesh-storage-health.XXXXXXXX)"; trap 'rm -rf "$_sb"' EXIT INT HUP TERM
    mkdir -p "$_sb/.mesh"
    if HOME="$_sb" "$_ds" >/dev/null 2>&1 && [ -f "$_sb/.mesh/.disk-space.state" ]; then
      _w="$(read_capacity_where "$_sb/.mesh/.disk-space.state")"
      [ -n "$_w" ] || { echo "  FAIL cross-tool: mesh-disk-space's live state names no mount this parser can read: $(head -1 "$_sb/.mesh/.disk-space.state")"; fail=1; }
      case "$_w" in /*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL cross-tool: parsed mount is not a path: '$_w'"; fail=1 ;; esac
    else
      echo "  (cross-tool format gate skipped: mesh-disk-space produced no state in the sandbox)"
    fi
    rm -rf "$_sb"; trap - EXIT INT HUP TERM
  else
    echo "  (cross-tool format gate skipped: mesh-disk-space not present)"
  fi

  # --- LIVE-PATH ROUND TRIP for the persistence counter. The classifier above proves the RULE;
  # it proves nothing about the counter being CARRIED across runs — exactly the gap that makes an
  # escalation rule a decoration. Drive the real read/write path in a sandbox HOME (never the live
  # state — a --test must not write the artifact a human or watchdog reads for liveness).
  _pb="$(mktemp -d -t mesh-storage-health-run.XXXXXXXX)"; mkdir -p "$_pb/.mesh"
  printf 'NORMAL|use_pct=18|inode_pct=2|writeable=yes|space OK (use=18%% on / inode=2%% on /)\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.disk-space.state"
  printf 'STALLED|verdict=STALLED|dev=dm-0|side=write|write_ms=1400\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.disk-latency-state"
  printf 'COOL|40850|40.9|89850|94850|x|x\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.nvme-temp.state"
  printf 'AGG:HEALTHY|devices=2|x\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.nvme-health.state"
  # run 1: no prior state -> stall run 1 -> DEGRADED (media contradicts)
  _r1="$(HOME="$_pb" MESH_DIR="$_pb/.mesh" "$0" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$_r1" in *DEGRADED*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL live-path run1: expected DEGRADED on a first STALLED against healthy media, got: $_r1"; fail=1;; esac
  grep -q '|stall=1$' "$_pb/.mesh/.storage-health-state" || { echo "  FAIL live-path: stall counter not persisted after run 1: $(cat "$_pb/.mesh/.storage-health-state")"; fail=1; }
  # runs 2..STALL_ESCALATE: the counter must CARRY and escalate exactly at the bound
  _i=2
  while [ "$_i" -le "$STALL_ESCALATE" ]; do
    _rn="$(HOME="$_pb" MESH_DIR="$_pb/.mesh" "$0" 2>/dev/null)"; _i=$((_i + 1))
  done
  case "$_rn" in *CRITICAL*persisted*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL live-path escalation: $STALL_ESCALATE consecutive STALLED did not re-raise CRITICAL, got: $_rn"; fail=1;; esac
  # a single recovered eval must RESET the run — otherwise the counter ratchets and every later
  # stall escalates instantly, which is the same false incident wearing a new mechanism.
  printf 'NORMAL|verdict=NORMAL|dev=dm-0|side=write|write_ms=52\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.disk-latency-state"
  HOME="$_pb" MESH_DIR="$_pb/.mesh" "$0" >/dev/null 2>&1
  grep -q '|stall=0$' "$_pb/.mesh/.storage-health-state" || { echo "  FAIL live-path: a recovered eval did not reset the stall run: $(cat "$_pb/.mesh/.storage-health-state")"; fail=1; }
  printf 'STALLED|verdict=STALLED|dev=dm-0|side=write|write_ms=1400\n' > "$_pb/.mesh/.disk-latency-state"
  _rr="$(HOME="$_pb" MESH_DIR="$_pb/.mesh" "$0" 2>/dev/null)"
  case "$_rr" in *DEGRADED*) : ;; *) echo "  FAIL live-path: after a reset the next STALLED escalated immediately, got: $_rr"; fail=1;; esac
  rm -rf "$_pb"

  [ "$fail" = 1 ] && { echo "smoke-test: FAIL"; exit 1; }
  echo "smoke-test: ok (fusion assertions: CRITICAL x3+priority, DEGRADED attribution x6+alone x3, HEALTHY x3, OFFLINE x3, mount-attribution x4, media-axis x16 incl. the stall/media asymmetry + persistence boundary, + live cross-tool format gates for disk-space AND nvme-health + the media staleness bound; + COLD START: abstention vs a genuinely dead feeder, self-expiry at the staleness window, the read-helper WIRING over real mtimes, and the reason prose checked against mesh-dash's own alarm regex with a control arm; + no unrecognised axis value may reach an all-clear)"
  exit 0
fi

# --- Live read path ---
cap="$(read_capacity "$MESH/.disk-space.state")"
capw="$(read_capacity_where "$MESH/.disk-space.state")"
lat="$(read_latency "$MESH/.disk-latency-state")"
therm="$(read_thermal "$MESH/.nvme-temp.state")"
media="$(read_media "$MESH/.nvme-health.state")"

# capture prev BEFORE overwriting STATE — reading it after the write would always see the label
# we just wrote (a real latent bug in the sibling mesh-system-vitality, which does exactly that
# and so its --edge is a permanent no-op); do the read/write in the right order here.
prev=""; prev_stall=0
if [ -f "$STATE" ]; then
  _prev_line="$(head -1 "$STATE" 2>/dev/null || true)"
  prev="$(printf '%s' "$_prev_line" | cut -d'|' -f1)"
  # stall= is a trailing field appended by this tool; an OLD state file written before it existed
  # has none, which correctly reads 0 (a fresh run cannot claim a history it never observed).
  prev_stall="$(printf '%s' "$_prev_line" | sed -n 's/.*|stall=\([0-9]\+\).*/\1/p')"
  [ -n "$prev_stall" ] || prev_stall=0
fi
# consecutive STALLED run, this eval included. Any non-STALLED reading resets it — the escalation
# is about a stall that HELD, so one recovered eval genuinely ends the run.
if [ "$lat" = "STALLED" ]; then stallrun=$((prev_stall + 1)); else stallrun=0; fi

result="$(classify_storage "$cap" "$lat" "$therm" "$capw" "$media" "$stallrun")"
label="${result%%|*}"
reason="${result#*|}"

TS=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
mkdir -p "$MESH" 2>/dev/null || true
printf '%s|capacity=%s|latency=%s|thermal=%s|media=%s|%s|stall=%s\n' "$label" "$cap" "$lat" "$therm" "$media" "$reason" "$stallrun" > "$STATE"
[ -x "$HOME/.local/bin/mesh-state-touch" ] && mesh-state-touch "$STATE" >/dev/null 2>&1 || touch "$STATE" 2>/dev/null || true

# --- Output ---
# THE TIMESTAMP LEADS, and it is not cosmetic. `--edge` is cron-wired with its stdout appended to
# ~/.mesh/storage-health.log, which made that log the tool's own tape — and until 2026-08-26 no row
# on it carried a `t` of any kind, so the tape structurally could not test the claim its own verdict
# text makes: "thermal=HOT (leading indicator)" needs HOT at t before SLOW at t+k, and there was no
# t. (The defect was only testable at all because chat.log timestamps the board edges; the answer
# was 0 for 15 — task nvme-hot-constant-shadows-the-drives-own-published-ceiling.) It leads rather
# than trails because `reason` is FREE TEXT that ends the line: a field appended after free text is
# re-parsed by whatever the free text happens to contain, so the only position a reader can trust
# is ahead of it. Nothing anchors on '^[storage-health' (checked across scripts/ and ~/.local/bin).
if [ "${1:-}" = --json ]; then
  printf '{"label":"%s","reason":"%s","inputs":{"capacity":"%s","latency":"%s","thermal":"%s","media":"%s"},"stall_run":%s,"ts":"%s"}\n' \
    "$label" "$reason" "$cap" "$lat" "$therm" "$media" "$stallrun" "$TS"
  exit 0
fi
if [ "${1:-}" = --edge ]; then
  [ "$prev" = "$label" ] && exit 0
  printf '%s [storage-health-%s] %s — %s\n' "$TS" "$(printf '%s' "$label" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')" "$label" "$reason"
  # A state CHANGE that only prints to cron stdout is a SILENT alert — invisible to the mesh.
  # (health flagged 2026-07-31: storage DEGRADED 30min+, verdict never reached chat.log, only
  # visible via mesh-dash/direct run.) Land the edge on the board so a storage transition is
  # SEEN by every mind. CRITICAL = imminent failure -> incident [task] for the health owner;
  # DEGRADED / recovery -> [fyi] naming the transition. Edge-gated (fires only on prev!=label)
  # so it cannot spam. NOT emitted in --test (that would forge a board post — a --test must never
  # write the log a human/watchdog reads); the live --edge path is the only writer.
  if command -v mesh-chat >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    from="${prev:-none}"
    case "$label" in
      CRITICAL) edge_msg="[task] storage-health CRITICAL on $(hostname): $reason (owner: health priority:incident)" ;;
      *)        edge_msg="[fyi] storage-health $(hostname): ${from}->${label} — $reason" ;;
    esac
    MESH_WHO="storage-health@$(hostname)" mesh-chat "$edge_msg" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
  fi
  exit 0
fi
printf '%s [storage-health-%s] %s — %s\n' "$TS" "$(printf '%s' "$label" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')" "$label" "$reason"
